From 21572ca379e17ea2a02c91f50db7943a8d08ecd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 13:27:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] utils: do not include <malloc.h>, provide memalign() on
 Darwin

<malloc.h> is a glibc header. Darwin does not have it, so every file
that includes it fails before anything else is compiled:

  common/utils/utils.h:16:10: fatal error: 'malloc.h' file not found

Of the fifteen files that include it, none uses anything from it except
memalign(), reached through the malloc16() macros in utils.h,
defs_nr_UE.h and defs_L1_NB_IoT.h and through a handful of direct calls
in the PHY. Twelve files only need <stdlib.h> and get that instead.
utils.h, which every memalign() user reaches through tools_defs.h or
log.h, keeps <malloc.h> off Darwin and defines memalign() there as an
inline wrapper over posix_memalign(), the portable spelling of the same
allocation. The two PHY headers that spell out malloc16() over
memalign() keep <malloc.h> under #ifndef __APPLE__ and rely on utils.h
on Darwin.
